<div class="ct-container" style="padding: 40px 24px 70px;">
  <div style="font-family: inherit; color: #1e293b; line-height: 1.7; max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto;">
    <h1 style="color: #0D529C; font-size: 26px; font-weight: 800; margin-bottom: 16px;">How Do Modern Digital Hearing Aids Work?</h1>
    <p style="font-size: 16px; color: #475569;">
      Unlike old analog amplifiers that simply made all sound louder (including painful background traffic and fan noise), modern digital hearing aids function like high-speed miniature computers inside your ear.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color: #0D529C; font-size: 22px; font-weight: 700; margin-top: 32px;">The 4-Stage Acoustic Process</h2>
    <ol style="padding-left: 22px; color: #334155; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;">
      <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">
        <strong>Microphone Pickup:</strong> Directional dual microphones capture acoustic sound waves from your surrounding environment and convert them into electrical signals.
      </li>
      <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">
        <strong>Digital Signal Processing (DSP):</strong> The central processor analyzes sound hundreds of times per second. It separates human speech from background noise, suppresses wind turbulence, and amplifies only the specific frequencies where you have hearing deficits.
      </li>
      <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">
        <strong>Receiver / Speaker Delivery:</strong> The refined digital signal is converted back into acoustic sound waves and delivered precisely into the ear canal.
      </li>
      <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">
        <strong>Acoustic Neural Transmission:</strong> Your auditory nerve receives clear sound vibrations and transmits them to the brain for natural cognitive comprehension.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</div>

